The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ocfs2_inode_unlock((*ac)->ac_inode, 1);
inode_unlock((*ac)->ac_inode);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code attempts to unlock the global bitmap inode twice without ensuring it is locked first. If `ocfs2_reserve_cluster_bitmap_bits()` fails with ENOSPC, the code calls `ocfs2_inode_unlock()` and `inode_unlock()` on the same inode. If the retry fails and the inode was not locked, this results in a double unlock, leading to a kernel BUG.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
ret = ocfs2_inode_lock((*ac)->ac_inode, NULL, 1);
if (ret < 0) {
    mlog_errno(ret);
    inode_unlock((*ac)->ac_inode);
    iput((*ac)->ac_inode);
    (*ac)->ac_inode = NULL;
    goto bail;
}
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that the inode is properly locked before any unlock operations are performed. By checking the return value of `ocfs2_inode_lock()` and handling the error case appropriately, the code prevents the scenario where an unlock is attempted on an inode that was never locked, thus avoiding the double unlock issue.

